\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Example}
\title{Reward hacking under an incomplete checker}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{reward-hacking}
\tag{evaluation}

\p{Shifting probability mass toward a checker exploit raises proxy reward
while reducing independently judged utility.}

\tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, align=center, font=\small,
    text width=28mm, minimum height=10mm},
  flow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node[box] (outputs) at (0,0) {outputs\\$41$, exact $42$, padded $42$};
\node[box] (checker) at (4.0,1.25) {proxy checker\\contains $42$};
\node[box] (evaluator) at (4.0,-1.25) {independent rule\\equals $42$};
\node[box] (contrast) at (8.0,0) {proxy $.5\to.9$\\utility $.4\to.1$};
\draw[flow] (outputs) -- (checker);
\draw[flow] (outputs) -- (evaluator);
\draw[flow] (checker) -- (contrast);
\draw[flow] (evaluator) -- (contrast);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Let the three outputs be #{y_0=\text{41}},
#{y_1=\text{42}}, and #{y_2=\text{ignore; 42; done}}. The proxy accepts
#{y_1,y_2}; the independent evaluator accepts only #{y_1}. Moving their
probabilities from #{(0.5,0.4,0.1)} to #{(0.1,0.1,0.8)} changes expected
proxy reward from #{0.5} to #{0.9}, but true utility from #{0.4} to #{0.1}.}

\p{Reward exploitation in formal verification is discussed in
\citet{Section 3.2}{ren2025deepseekproverv2}. The constructed checker exposes
one failure mechanism. It provides no basis for treating every executable
verifier as incomplete or every proxy improvement as reward hacking.}
